Weekly Progress Log – Week 3/9

Project: Interactive Desktop Companion Robot
Team: Jiajun Gao, Yuchen Shih, Zichao Wang

This Week’s Progress

This week, we focused on building and validating a breadboard-based prototype demo for the motion and safety subsystems of our robot.

1. Remote Motor Control via Web Interface

We successfully implemented a basic wireless control system using a mobile web interface. The robot connects to Wi-Fi and receives control commands from a browser-based controller.

The following motion commands were implemented:

Move forward
Turn left
Turn right
Stop

This functionality verifies the end-to-end communication pipeline between the user interface and the ESP32-based motor control system, and confirms correct integration with the motor driver.

2. Safety Sensing Implementation (Dual Sensor Logic)

To align with our system’s safety requirements (e.g., obstacle avoidance and desk-edge detection), we implemented two sensor-based safety mechanisms:

(a) Front Obstacle Detection
A front-facing distance sensor detects nearby obstacles.
When an object is detected within a threshold distance, the robot immediately stops.
This ensures basic obstacle avoidance behavior.
(b) Downward Desk-Edge Detection
A downward-facing sensor monitors the distance to the surface below the robot.
If the detected distance exceeds a threshold (approximately 7 cm), the system assumes the robot is at the edge of a desk.
In this case, all motor outputs are immediately disabled to prevent falling.
The robot is only allowed to move when a valid surface is detected.

This logic directly supports the fail-safe safety mechanism described in our design, where sensor input can override motor commands to ensure safe operation.

Key Achievements
Verified real-time Wi-Fi control → ESP32 → motor driver pipeline
Successfully demonstrated basic mobility control
Implemented real-time safety overrides using sensor feedback
Built a working breadboard prototype for rapid testing

Challenges
